## How It Works

Concise technical explanation of the mechanism.

## Key Points

- **DP State**: local[k], global[k] track ending vs non-ending subarrays
- **Transition**: local[k] = max(local[k]+a[i], global[k-1]+a[i]); global[k] = max(global[k], local[k])
- **Edge Handling**: cap K to n; initialize -inf; handles negative values

## Example

```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, K) {
  const n = A.length;
  if (n === 0 || K <= 0) return 0;
  K = Math.min(K, n);
  const global = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  const local = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  global[0] = 0;
  for (let x of A) {
    for (let k = K; k >= 1; k--) {
      local[k] = Math.max((local[k] !== -Infinity ? local[k] + x : -Infinity), global[k-1] + x);
      global[k] = Math.max(global[k], local[k]);
    }
  }
  return global[K];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests concrete DP skills on string partitioning with reconstruction; approachable for beginners yet non-trivial.

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t]: maximum sum for first i digits split into t parts
- val(j,i): integer value of s[j..i-1] (leading zeros allowed)
- transition: dp[i][t] = max_{j ∈ [t-1..i-1]} dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i)
- reconstruction: track prev to recover split indices
- complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space; use BigInt for large results

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSplit(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const val = (a,b)=> BigInt(s.slice(a,b));
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1n));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    dp[i][1] = val(0,i);
  }

  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<i;j++){
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i);
        if(candidate > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct splits
  const splits = [];
  let curr = n, parts = k;
  while(parts > 1){
    const j = prev[curr][parts];
    splits.push(j);
    curr = j;
    parts--;
  }
  splits.reverse();

  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], splits};
}
```
